I guess you are damned if you do, damned if you don't. :)

>>
>> Note 6:
>>
>> Some thought seems to have gone into SpatialPolygonDataFrames visavi
>> the fortify methods. Wouldn't it be more elegant for geom_map to
>> operate directly on SpatialPolygonDataFrames objects passed through
>> the data parameter. This would eliminate the need to keep track of two
>> data objects, and the key between them (map_id).
>
> That assumes that SpatialPolygonDataFrames is the most common map data
> format.  I'm not sure it is - but you're right that that code would be
> more efficient if it didn't have to coerce everything into a data
> frame (a la annotate_raster)

I don't see it as assuming that. Even if the data needs to be
fortified into a Spatial*DataFrame, the advantage is elegance. With
spatial dataframes there is:
1. no question about whether the keys between two dataframes match
2. you can subset easily
3. The native coordinate system is stored with the data

I want to be able to write something like:

ggplot(data=spdata,aes(fill=variable)) +
  annotate_open_street_map(zoom=2) +
  geom_spatial_polygon(alpha=0.5) +
  coord_map(projection=CRS("+proj=merc +a=6378137 +b=6378137
+lat_ts=0.0 +lon_0=0.0 +x_0=0.0 +y_0=0
+k=1.0 +units=m +nadgrids=@null +no_defs"))

 and get a nice choropleth with an open street map raster image in the
background. In terms of elegant grammar, this feels like the way to go
for me. I can contribute the "annotate_open_street_map" if the
framework is there (i've done some work with OSM rasters
http://blog.fellstat.com/?p=20 ).

>
>> Note 7:
>>
