Just to be clear, here is an example:

pdf()
ggplot() +
	geom_dotplot(aes(x = women),data=Prestige,alpha = 0.5)
dev.off()

this uses all the default settings, but more than just the outline is
overlapping. If the dotsize parameter is changed, then things start to
get very crazy.

Ian

On Tue, Feb 21, 2012 at 8:19 AM, Winston Chang <winsto...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> Note 10:
>>
>> in geom_dotplot, I seem to be getting more overlapping than I would
>> expect. How much overlapping should be apparent when looking at the
>> points with alpha=.5?
>
>
> It's hard to say without seeing an example with data, but I can think of a
> few possibilities.
>
> The dots are drawn with a fill and a outline (controlled with 'colour'). I
> added the outline because if you use just a fill, the result is jagged on a
> number of bitmap devices -- we've probably all seen this with geom_point. If
> you're using small points, the result is that the outline can make the
> entire dot slightly larger, leading to overlap. You can make the outline go
> away with colour=NA.
>
> The other reason has to do with the way binning works. If you
> use geom_dotplot(binmethod="histodot"). But if you use the default
> "dotdensity" method, it can result in a fair bit of overlap, up to 1/2 the
> dot diameter. I've attached an example with values c(0.49, 1.4, 1.45, 1.5,
> 3, 4), and binwidth=1. The vertical lines mark where each data point is. The
> output in this case is not a great representation of the data, but this is
> the result of the binning algorithm from Wilkinson's dot plot paper.
>
> In that paper, he describes a smoothing algorithm which can help reduce
> left-right bias (and possibly overlap). I made an attempt at implementing
> it, but either I didn't understand it, or there was an error in the
> description of the method. If someone would like to enlighten me, the paper
> is here:
> www.cs.uic.edu/~wilkinson/Publications/dots.pdf <http://www.cs.uic.edu/~wilkinson/Publications/dots.pdf>
>
> -Winston

